  "Question": "Given \\(x^2-16=(x+4)(x-a)\\), find the value of \\(a\\).",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "4",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Use the difference of squares to expose the factor shared by both sides:\n\\[\nx^2-16=x^2-4^2=(x+4)(x-4).\n\\]\nIf \\(x\\ne-4\\), the factor \\(x+4\\) is nonzero, so canceling it in the given equality gives \\(x-4=x-a\\), hence \\(a=4\\). If \\(x=-4\\), both sides equal \\(0\\) for every \\(a\\).\n\nIf the equality is intended as a polynomial identity, meaning it holds for every \\(x\\), it must also hold when \\(x\\ne-4\\), so the answer is \\(a=4\\). Without this interpretation or the condition \\(x\\ne-4\\), \\(a\\) is not uniquely determined.",
